WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.202
Recall at k is one of the easiest retrieval metrics to understand.

00:00:04.202 --> 00:00:13.087
For each evaluation question, we retrieve the top k chunks and check whether the expected source document

00:00:13.087 --> 00:00:15.287
appears anywhere in that set.

00:00:15.287 --> 00:00:20.722
If the correct document is present, that question is a hit.

00:00:20.722 --> 00:00:24.272
Recall at k is the proportion of questions that produce a hit.

00:00:24.272 --> 00:00:30.462
[tts-break] Recall at one is stricter because the correct document must be ranked first.

00:00:30.462 --> 00:00:34.772
Recall at three gives the system a little more room.

00:00:34.772 --> 00:00:41.087
This metric does not tell us everything, but it answers an important question:

00:00:41.087 --> 00:00:44.017
did retrieval provide the evidence we needed?

00:00:44.017 --> 00:00:49.392
If recall is poor, changing the language model is usually not the first fix.

00:00:49.392 --> 00:00:54.192
Investigate chunking, metadata, embeddings, filters, and retrieval methods first.

00:00:54.192 --> 00:00:59.676
This is how evaluation helps us target the correct layer.
